Hi Kenny,


Yes, it is. SMF round-robins UPF list associated with PFCP. See the following code.
```
// src/smf/context.c:630

static ogs_pfcp_node_t *selected_upf_node(
        ogs_pfcp_node_t *current, smf_sess_t *sess)
{
    ogs_pfcp_node_t *next, *node;

    ogs_assert(current);
    ogs_assert(sess);

    next = ogs_list_next(current);
    for (node = next; node; node = ogs_list_next(node)) {
        if (OGS_FSM_CHECK(&node->sm, smf_pfcp_state_associated) &&
            compare_ue_info(node, sess) == true) return node;
    }

    for (node = ogs_list_first(&ogs_pfcp_self()->peer_list);
            node != next; node = ogs_list_next(node)) {
        if (OGS_FSM_CHECK(&node->sm, smf_pfcp_state_associated) &&
            compare_ue_info(node, sess) == true) return node;
    }

    return next ? next : ogs_list_first(&ogs_pfcp_self()->peer_list);
}
```

So, you can see the OGS_FSM_CHECK(state machine, PFCP associated) in the above. SGW-C is also using same method.

Let me know if you have any further questions.

Thanks a lot!
Sukchan
